5, W6, W7]` or `[W6, W7, W8]`.\n4.  **Discard B7**: This leaves a single `B7` in hand.\n5.  **Win on B7**: Wait for any player to discard `B7` to win.\n    - **Scoring**: Melded Hand (6) + One Voided Suit (1) + No Honors (1) + Single Wait (1) = **9 points**. (Valid Win).\n\nDiscarding B8 is the most efficient move to facilitate this setup. B8 is also relatively safe given the `Next` player's discard history of Dots.\n\nEvaluation: Discarding B8 maintains the most efficient path to a winning hand while setting up for necessary point requirements. The current hand needs more points to reach the 8-point minimum. By keeping the W6-W7 shape, the player can call 'Chi' on W5 or W8 to expose the final meld, then discard a B7 to enter a single wait for the last B7. This sequence secures the \"Melded Hand\" (6 pts) pattern, which combined with \"No Honors\" (1 pt), \"One Voided Suit\" (1 pt), and \"Single Wait\" (1 pt) totals 9 points. Discarding B8 is also safer than breaking the W shape or the B pair.\n\nReason: The hand currently lacks sufficient points (only ~2-3) to win simply by completing a sequence. To win, the player should aim for the \"Melded Hand\" pattern (6 pts) by exposing the last meld. Discarding B8 keeps the W6-W7 shape ready to Chi, and prepares the hand to transition to a single wait on B7, satisfying the 8-point threshold.\n\nAnswer: Play B8"}
